  "hints": [
    "Separate the problem into two independent decisions: which words end up on a line, and how the spaces inside that line are arranged.",
    "For the line decision, add the next word while its length plus one space per existing gap still fits within maxWidth. There is no trade-off to explore, the greedy run is forced.",
    "For the arrangement, leftover = maxWidth - (sum of the run's lengths) is divided over words - 1 gaps: base = leftover // gaps everywhere, plus one extra space for each of the first leftover % gaps gaps. The last line and single-word lines are left-aligned and padded instead."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force decides each line by guessing from the far end: for the current start it sets the tentative end to the last word in the array and walks that end leftwards, rescanning the whole candidate run each time, until the run fits in maxWidth; the surviving run is the greedy line. It then pads by handing out one space at a time in round-robin fashion over the gaps until the leftover is exhausted. The optimal walks the end pointer forward instead, carrying the accumulated width of the current run, and stops as soon as adding the next word (plus one space per existing gap) would pass maxWidth; each word is therefore visited a constant number of times across the whole layout. Padding is then computed arithmetically: base = leftover // gaps spaces for every gap and one extra space for the first leftover % gaps gaps.",
    "why_optimal": "Line breaking is inherently sequential and greedy: the first line's run is forced because a word cannot be moved to the next line to make the current line fit better without breaking the order, so a single left-to-right pass is enough and the O(n^2) rescanning of the brute force is pure waste. Space usage is dominated by the answer itself, n * maxWidth characters in the worst case (at most 2000 here), and the arithmetic distribution writes each gap in one operation, so the whole task is linear in the size of the output it must produce. Nothing faster is meaningful: you cannot lay out fewer than the characters you have to return.",
    "pitfalls": "The last line and any single-word line must be left-aligned and padded with single spaces, not distributed; mixing the two rules produces lines of the wrong length. Distributing with leftover // gaps alone drops the remainder and silently shortens lines, so the first leftover % gaps gaps each need one extra space, leftmost first. Do not use words.length - 1 as a divisor on a single-word line (division by zero). Counting the gaps as the number of words rather than words - 1 shifts every space count by one. Every returned string must measure exactly maxWidth characters, including the final one, which many implementations forget to pad at all. The packing loop must also consume at least one word per iteration: a word longer than maxWidth leaves the greedy run empty, so the loop needs an explicit fallback that puts that single word on its own line, otherwise the line start never moves and the output grows without bound. Clamp every padding count at zero for the same reason."
  },
